239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 194 0 R
/T (The studio began with an exploration of the Luddite Movement and its relationship with technology. During the Industrial Revolution, machines were introduced into textile production and started replacing the work of skilled workers. Many workers feared of losing their jobs and livelihoods because of these machines. In response, they destroyed machines as a form of protest.)
/E (The studio began with an exploration of the Luddite Movement and its relationship with technology. During the Industrial Revolution, machines were introduced into textile production and started replacing the work of skilled workers. Many workers feared of losing their jobs and livelihoods because of these machines. In response, they destroyed machines as a form of protest.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 194 0 R
/T (While learning about the Luddites, I started thinking about what happens when technology enters an existing system and changes the way people work.  I am interested in what happens to the people who are already part of a system when new technology changes it. This led me to think about a simple question: When technology changes a system, who benefits and who gets left behind?)
/E (While learning about the Luddites, I started thinking about what happens when technology enters an existing system and changes the way people work.  I am interested in what happens to the people who are already part of a system when new technology changes it. This led me to think about a simple question: When technology changes a system, who benefits and who gets left behind?)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 194 0 R
/T (I then started looking at this question through something much closer to everyday life. I looked at the shift from offline shopping to quick commerce. Earlier, buying groceries usually meant going to a nearby local shop, talking to the shopkeeper and buying what was needed. Now, with quick commerce platforms, the same things can be ordered through a phone and delivered within minutes. This change in shopping much faster and more convenient, but it also made me think about what happens to the local shops that were already part of this system.)
/E (I then started looking at this question through something much closer to everyday life. I looked at the shift from offline shopping to quick commerce. Earlier, buying groceries usually meant going to a nearby local shop, talking to the shopkeeper and buying what was needed. Now, with quick commerce platforms, the same things can be ordered through a phone and delivered within minutes. This change in shopping much faster and more convenient, but it also made me think about what happens to the local shops that were already part of this system.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 194 0 R
/T (I started noticing that the change was not only about convenience. It also had social, political and economic effects. Small retailers could be affected as people's shopping habits changed and more people started ordering through digital platforms. There was also a question of how large quick commerce companies and small local shops could compete fairly. Economically, things like price competition, changing retail practices and minimum order conditions for free delivery could also affect small businesses.)
/E (I started noticing that the change was not only about convenience. It also had social, political and economic effects. Small retailers could be affected as people's shopping habits changed and more people started ordering through digital platforms. There was also a question of how large quick commerce companies and small local shops could compete fairly. Economically, things like price competition, changing retail practices and minimum order conditions for free delivery could also affect small businesses.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 194 0 R
/T (This brought me back to the Luddites. The Luddites had used the hammer to resist machines that they felt were threatening their livelihoods. I started asking myself, if technology is the machine of our time, then what could be the new hammer in the server room?)
/E (This brought me back to the Luddites. The Luddites had used the hammer to resist machines that they felt were threatening their livelihoods. I started asking myself, if technology is the machine of our time, then what could be the new hammer in the server room?)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 194 0 R
/T (For me, the answer was collaboration. Instead of thinking about technology as something that has to replace the existing system, I started thinking about whether it could work with the people who are already part of that system. In the case of quick commerce, I imagined a model where local grocery stores could become part of the delivery network instead of being pushed out by it. Technology could then support the existing businesses rather than simply replacing them.)
/E (For me, the answer was collaboration. Instead of thinking about technology as something that has to replace the existing system, I started thinking about whether it could work with the people who are already part of that system. In the case of quick commerce, I imagined a model where local grocery stores could become part of the delivery network instead of being pushed out by it. Technology could then support the existing businesses rather than simply replacing them.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 195 0 R
/T (Critical Making: Conceptual and Material Studies in Technology and Social Life by Matt Ratto. )
/E (Critical Making: Conceptual and Material Studies in Technology and Social Life by Matt Ratto. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
246 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (After exploring the Luddites, we moved towards Critical Making. We read Critical Making: Conceptual and Material Studies in Technology and Social Life by Matt Ratto. This made me start thinking about making not only as a way of producing something, but also as a way of understanding an idea through the process of making.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Along with the reading, we visited the Art and Science Lab in our college. There, I saw 3D printing and different kinds of technical making happening around me. For me, this was very new. I had never done 3D printing or worked with electronics before. I remember feeling very confused while looking at the machines and the process. I did not really understand what was happening there, and at that point I was quite sure that I would never choose 3D printing for my own work.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (At that stage, I was also thinking about what kind of making I was comfortable with. Coming from a visual design background, working with electronics, machines and 3D printing felt very different from what I had done before. I was more used to working with graphic design and visual outcomes, so this kind of technical making felt unfamiliar to me.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Protocol: How Control Exists After Decentralization by Alexander Galloway.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (After the Critical Making reading and the visit to the Art and Science Lab, we started reading book called Protocol: How Control Exists After Decentralization by Alexander Galloway. The book was divided among 14 people, and I have choosen the chapter Tactical Media.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (When I started reading the chapter, I understood Tactical Media through the way people use technology and existing systems to question or challenge how those systems work. The chapter talks about different examples from digital media, including hackers, computer viruses, cyberfeminism and online protests that temporarily overload or block access to a website. What I found interesting was that these examples were not simply about rejecting technology. They were about finding ways to work within the same systems and use their own structures differently.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (From Tactical Media, I started seeing a different way of thinking. Instead of simply breaking or rejecting the technology, people could use the same technology in a different way to question what it is doing.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (At this point, I still had no idea what I wanted to do for my final project. I was mainly trying to understand how technology works within systems and how people can find different ways of using those systems.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (The idea that stayed with me was that technology does not always have to be accepted exactly as it is given to us. The way we use it, respond to it and change our relationship with it can also be questioned. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 196 0 R
/T (A1 - From Exploration of Ideas to Final Idea Selection)
/E (A1 - From Exploration of Ideas to Final Idea Selection)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (After the earlier explorations around Luddite Movement, Critical Making and Protocol I started noticing for a protocol that I could observe in everyday life and work. I had a few different directions in mind, and I was trying to understand which one I was more interested in exploring.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (One of the idea I had was quick commerce. I had already looked at the shift from local shops to online delivery and the effect this change could have on small retailers. I was interested in how a new digital system can change an existing way of doing things. I had also started thinking about how technology could work along with local shops instead of replacing them.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (Another direction I thought about was the shift from handwriting to digital writing. I started noticing how children today are becoming more used to typing and writing digitally instead of writing by hand. I was interested in how this change is slowly becoming a normal part of everyday life. It made me think about how easily we adopt a new system when it becomes more convenient, and what might happen to the older habits that we leave behind.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (I also had an idea around invisible influence. I started thinking about how digital platforms can quietly shape our everyday choices. We often feel that we are making our own decisions, but the things we see on platforms, the recommendations we receive and the way information is presented can influence what we choose.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (Along with this, I was also thinking about the influence of the people around us. The people we live with, work with or spend time with can also affect the decisions we make)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (without us always noticing it. I was interested in this idea of how our everyday choices are shaped by things around us, even when we feel that the choice is completely our own.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 197 0 R
/T (I had these different directions, but I realised that I was most interested in something that I experienced very directly in my own everyday life. I started looking more closely at my relationship with my phone. I noticed how a notification could interrupt whatever I was doing. I could be working, studying or doing something else, and a small sound from my phone could immediately make me look at it.)
/E (I had these different directions, but I realised that I was most interested in something that I experienced very directly in my own everyday life. I started looking more closely at my relationship with my phone. I noticed how a notification could interrupt whatever I was doing. I could be working, studying or doing something else, and a small sound from my phone could immediately make me look at it.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 197 0 R
/T (This felt different from the other ideas because I could clearly observe it happening around me. I started paying attention to what happens in that small moment: I am doing something, the phone gives me a notification, I notice it, and my attention moves towards the phone.)
/E (This felt different from the other ideas because I could clearly observe it happening around me. I started paying attention to what happens in that small moment: I am doing something, the phone gives me a notification, I notice it, and my attention moves towards the phone.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 197 0 R
/T (I became interested in this shift of attention rather than the larger idea of phone usage. I was not trying to say that phones are bad or that people should stop using them. I wanted to understand this small everyday behaviour and what happens when a notification pulls us away from what we were already doing.)
/E (I became interested in this shift of attention rather than the larger idea of phone usage. I was not trying to say that phones are bad or that people should stop using them. I wanted to understand this small everyday behaviour and what happens when a notification pulls us away from what we were already doing.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
